WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
This video was sponsored by brilliant.org.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.020
Lasers have transformed science.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:10.740
Today, they're used in all manner of research from supercold atoms to astrophysics to biophysics.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.580
In fact the most recent Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.860
"for groundbreaking inventions in the field of laser physics".

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.940
What's unique about them is the fact that they produce light of one wavelength, which is coherent.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.140
That means that the crests of laser light waves are like marching soldiers

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.480
all moving together with the same speed and same constant separation from one another.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.800
This allows scientists to do really cool things

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.600
and the more powerful the laser,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.600
the cooler the things we can do.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.660
Modern research lasers are really impressive pieces of kit.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.480
Okay, not quite that impress-

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.439
This is the Central Laser Facility in Oxfordshire in the UK.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.440
The facility is used for a variety of research projects,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.980
including recreating the conditions inside stars

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.340
and even imaging the motions of individual electrons in chemical reactions.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.120
They have a variety of lasers,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.040
but in particular they have a hugely powerful one called Vulcan.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.080
Vulcan outputs so much energy that for a tiny fraction of a second,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.660
it's more than ten thousand times more powerful than the entire national grid.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.420
In fact it's so powerful

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.600
that the beam in Vulcan would melt the components that make up the laser

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.100
if the scientists who designed it hadn't used a clever trick.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.680
This trick is what allows modern research lasers to be as powerful as they are

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.120
and it's what won the most recent Nobel Prize in Physics.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.140
In this video, we're going to talk about how it works.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.500
We'll come back to this laser
and how it's so powerful later.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.240
Promise.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.440
To begin with, all lasers work by a process called stimulated emission.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.680
In fact that's where the word laser comes from.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.780
It's an acronym for "light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation".

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.100
Stimulated emission works by carefully selecting a material

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.420
known as a gain medium, which amplifies an initial pulse of light.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.340
In this material individual atoms can be in a ground state or in an excited state.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.360
There are no in-betweens.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.340
An atom is either excited or it's not.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.580
If the atom is excited

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.800
then it can jump down to the ground state

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.960
by emitting a flash of light called a photon.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.080
This flash of light has a very specific wavelength associated with it,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.740
unique to the material the atom is made of.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.240
And so it will be the same for every atom of that gain medium

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.040
that jumps from the excited state to the ground state.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.140
Lasers work because of an effect where an atom in an excited state

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.120
is much more likely to jump down to the ground state

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.500
and so emits that specific flash of light

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.040
when an identical flash of light passes nearby.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.960
This process then allows for a small initial flash of light

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.460
to pass through the gain medium and be amplified several times

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.800
by passing several excited atoms,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.320
causing them to jump down to the ground state

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.520
and emit their identical flashes of light,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.820
thus becoming a really strong signal.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.280
If you put a mirror at either end of the gain medium

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.980
then you can repeat this process as many times as you like,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.420
if you keep raising atoms into excited states

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.300
by for example an electric field,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.960
and then release the light at one end.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.420
That gives you a pulse of laser light.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:14.500
But it's only the first part of a modern research laser like Vulcan.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.120
Because while this initial laser signal is weak,

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.700
it can be amplified through other components.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.780
But, there's a problem.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.620
As I said before,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.460
if you try and amplify this signal up to the power output of Vulcan or the Death Star,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.140
then the density of energy that you're putting through the optical components

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.220
like lenses and amplifiers is enough to damage those components.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.700
So you just have to find a Nobel Prize-winning way around it.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.240
That way is called chirped pulse amplification.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.880
And this is how it works.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.700
The reason that the laser beam would melt the glass is

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.840
because it would deliver an incredible amount of energy in a very short time.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.400
When I say a very short time,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.040
I mean about 200 femtoseconds

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.020
or 0.2 million millionths of a second.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:01.040
Chirped pulse amplification works by, first,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.160
stretching the laser pulse to a million times its original length.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.200
So it now lasts a few nanoseconds.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:12.380
This stretched signal is then amplified, increasing the energy in the pulse

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:18.340
until the energy density of the pulse is just less than the threshold for damaging the components in the laser.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.540
Then, just before the beam hits its target,
in the final part of the laser,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.620
the pulse is compressed back to its original shorter length in time.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.200
The amount of energy in the pulse is still the same,
but it's now over a shorter period of time,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.040
so the energy density is much much higher.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:40.080
So you deliver to the target an energy density that is way higher than your optical components could tolerate.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.060
Think of how the Death Star fires.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.380
If it fired its main beam from within the space station,
it would damage itself.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.920
So instead, it combines several lower power laser beams into one massively powerful beam

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.740
just before it hits the target.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.740
Except, while the Death Star combines and focuses beams

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.700
which are spread out in space (pun intended),

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.920
modern research lasers combine
beams spread out in time.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.780
And while the Death Star does this through a focusing dish, a bit like a lens,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.920
research lasers accomplish the same thing through another standard piece of kit.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.060
Diffraction gratings break up lights,

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.620
diffracting - that is basically deflecting -

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.760
different wavelengths of light by different amounts.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.860
Red light gets deflected the most, and blue light the least.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.180
A bit like a prism.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:29.140
Except, remember how at the start of the video I said that light from a laser is all the same wavelength?

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:34.220
So if you put it through a diffraction grating, it's all going to be diffracted by the same amount, right?

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.800
Well, not quite.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.180
Because of a variety of physical processes
that literally have whole textbooks written about them,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:47.280
laser light actually occupies a very narrow band of wavelengths, not just one number.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:53.300
So when you put it through a diffraction grating, it does spread out in space ever so slightly.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:58.960
The fact that it gets spread out in space is crucial to the trick of chirped pulse amplification.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.060
Because, if you use a diffraction grating,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.020
and normally a reflecting diffraction grating is used,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.080
position it at an angle with some lenses,

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.500
another grating and a mirror,

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.880
you can create a pulse stretcher.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.760
The key part here is on the right.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.240
Notice how the longer wavelength - red - light travels a shorter distance

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.480
than the shorter wavelength - blue - light.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.600
Because all light travels at the same speed,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.020
this means that while the red and blue light arrived
at the same time in the laser pulse,

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.480
when they leave the system, the red light does so slightly ahead of the blue light.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.180
the duration of the pulse of light has increased

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.640
and its colours spread out like a rainbow in time.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.480
At this point you can amplify your signal,

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.220
which Vulcan does through neodymium glass amplifiers,

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:42.580
and then when you're done,

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:45.820
compress the pulse of light back to its original short duration

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.160
using a similar setup of diffraction gratings.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.220
But this time blue light has a shorter path to travel and the red light travels a longer path.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.640
So you undo the stretching process

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.720
and you're left with a short pulse of extremely amplified laser light.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.260
This whole process is extremely delicate and needs to be carefully monitored,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.960
which explains why Vulcan is such a huge piece of kit.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.500
But it allows it to generate incredible energy densities.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:17.900
In 2005 it won a Guinness World Record for being the most powerful focused laser system in the world,

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.860
generating a petawatt pulse.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.400
That's a billion million watts.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.380
And when I was there, they let me fire it.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.360
I'm about to fire one of the most powerful lasers in the world.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:30.600
And it's touchscreen.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.180
Commence primary ignition.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.440
That may have been a bit anticlimactic,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.500
but that was very cool for me.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.940
So modern research lasers are only possible because of chirped pulse amplification:

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.400
stretching out your
pulse of laser light before amplifying it,

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.880
and then re-compressing it back
to a short, incredibly intense pulse.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.740
By doing this, we can access incredibly high energy densities,

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:18.400
even bringing the physics of faraway stars to Earth for a fraction of a second.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.500
I was assured by the scientists at the research facility however,

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:28.080
that we are still a very very long way off the construction of this particular technological terror.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.080
Yeesh.

